course: dictionary
lesson: 14662
title: Preferentially: better treatment or more often
level: primary-6
stars: 3
explain: Preferentially comes from prefer, which means to like one thing more than another. The ending -ly makes it an adverb, so it tells you how something is done.
explain: «The club let its members book the pool preferentially, so everyone else had to wait.» The members were served first and got better treatment than others. That is the first meaning: in a way that favours one person or group over others.
explain: In science, preferentially means more often or more strongly than other choices. «In our test, the bees preferentially landed on the blue flowers, not the yellow ones.» The bees chose blue more often, so blue was their preference.
explain: To check your meaning, swap in prefer or more often. If the sentence still makes sense, you have it. Look for clues like first, better, special or chose.
fact: Preferentially means in a way that gives one person or thing better treatment than others.
fact: In science, preferentially means more often or more strongly than other choices.
fact: «Treated preferentially» means someone gets special, better treatment.
fact: «Preferentially chosen» means picked over the others.
fact: Preferentially is a formal word, so you will often meet it in reports, rules and talk about fairness.
exercise: «The club let its members book the pool preferentially.» What does preferentially mean here? | *In a way that gives members better treatment than others | In a noisy way | In a quick and careless way | In a way that closes the pool to everyone
exercise: «In our test, the bees preferentially landed on blue flowers.» What does preferentially suggest? | The bees could not see yellow | The bees landed on blue flowers by accident | *The bees chose blue flowers more often than other flowers | The bees were afraid of blue flowers
exercise: Ben says preferentially means the same as equally. Why is he wrong? | They mean exactly the same thing | *Equally means fair to all, but preferentially means one group gets better treatment | Preferentially means in a sad way | Preferentially means fairly to all
test: Which sentence shows someone being treated preferentially? | «The bus was late, so everyone waited in the rain.» | «The class painted pictures of the sea.» | *«The head teacher let the winners go first, before anyone else.» | «The rabbit hopped across the field.»
test: In science, what does preferentially mean? | *More often or more strongly than other choices | Without any reason | Only once, and never again | Very slowly
exam: The rule says Year 6 children are served preferentially at lunch. What does this mean? | *Year 6 children are given better treatment than the other year groups | Year 6 children must skip lunch | Year 6 children get the same treatment as everyone | Year 6 children eat lunch outside
